  {
    "episode_id": 72845,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72845.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 23,
    "turn_text": "Well, the Senate is a little different than the House. In the House, you have a Rules Committee. And the Rules Committee is very slanted towards the majority, probably 9 to 4. And they decide how long the debate will go on and how many amendments will be. In the Senate, theoretically, the hallmark of the Senate is you get to have lots of amendments, lots of debate. So there'll be a huge argument that occurs as to how many amendments will be allowed. It's a little different in the Senate than the majority and minority leader will work that out. and then members all of the members will have to kind of agree with that because everything in the Senate is done under unanimous consent and if a single member, you know, raises objection, then it makes it very, very difficult, especially have groups of members. So that's something that the majority later Senator Reid and Senator McConnell will work out and i think it's really important that we have the ability to offer a number of amendments this bill is a huge bill spends lots of lots of money and uh... it can be made better",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
